{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"05","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"Debugging With AI Assistance","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting debugging with ai assistance to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Write a verifiable debugging prompt that states the symptom and includes the relevant code, and contrast it with a prompt the AI can only guess at.","Classify each AI debugging suggestion as accept, modify, or reject using a match check, a scope check, and a browser verify check, recording the reason.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/04"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_calibration"]},"content":"# Debugging With AI Assistance\n\n![Debugging With AI Assistance textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-05-debugging-with-ai-assistance.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAI coding assistants are fast and confident, and that combination is dangerous for a beginner who cannot yet tell a correct suggestion from a plausible-sounding wrong one. An assistant will happily propose a server configuration for a problem that has no server, or rewrite your whole site to fix a single typo. The skill that matters is not getting answers from AI; it is judging them against the actual symptom in your code and recording which you accepted, modified, or rejected and why.\n\nThis section teaches you to debug with AI as a junior partner you supervise, not an oracle you obey. You will learn to write a prompt that includes the real symptom and code, to verify a suggested fix instead of assuming it, and to keep a decision log that makes your judgment auditable. That log is the artifact, because it shows you understood the bug, not just that the bug went away.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write a verifiable debugging prompt that states the symptom and includes the relevant code, and contrast it with a prompt the AI can only guess at.\n- Classify each AI debugging suggestion as accept, modify, or reject using a match check, a scope check, and a browser verify check, recording the reason.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student's nav looks fine, but every paragraph below it renders blue and underlined, as if the whole page had become one link. He pastes \"fix my website\" into an AI assistant. It returns three suggestions: close an unclosed anchor tag, add a CORS server configuration for his headshot image, and rewrite the whole site in a JavaScript framework. All three sound technical and authoritative. He has no way to tell that only the first one is right, that the second is impossible for a local image, and that the third is wildly out of scope, so he tries them in order and makes the site worse before he makes it better.\n\nThe failure was not the AI; it was the absence of a method for evaluating what the AI said. This section gives you that method.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEffective AI-assisted debugging has three moving parts.\n\n**Read the symptom in the code first.** Before asking anyone (human or AI), locate the evidence. Blue runaway styling that swallows everything below a link is the classic signature of an unclosed `<a>` tag: the browser keeps treating later content as part of the link. Identifying the symptom yourself is what lets you judge whether a suggested fix actually matches it.\n\n**Write a verifiable prompt.** \"Fix my site\" gives the assistant nothing to verify against, so it guesses. A verifiable prompt states the symptom, includes the relevant code, and asks a specific question: \"Every paragraph below my nav renders blue and underlined as if it were a link. Here is my nav block and the first paragraph. What in my markup causes this?\" A prompt grounded in the symptom and the code produces suggestions you can check against reality.\n\n**Classify every suggestion: accept, modify, or reject, with a reason.** An assistant produces a mix of right, half-right, and irrelevant ideas. The discipline is to judge each one against the evidence. Accept a fix that matches the symptom. Modify a fix that is close but wrong in detail. Reject a fix that cannot apply (a same-folder image loaded by a relative path is same-origin, so [CORS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS), which governs cross-origin requests, does not apply) or that is out of scope (rewriting everything to fix one tag). The reason is what proves the judgment was yours.\n\nWhether the assistant is GitHub Copilot Chat in VS Code, ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini, the method is the same. Model versions and behavior change frequently, so what transfers is the supervision method (read the symptom, write a verifiable prompt, classify with a reason, verify in the browser), not the specific tool.\n\nThis is where [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students) becomes concrete: using AI responsibly means verifying its output, not laundering it as your own work. [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined) applies because professionals are expected to supervise tools, owning the result whether the suggestion came from a colleague, a search, or a model.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your headshot is a same-folder local file. An AI assistant confidently suggests adding a CORS server configuration to make it display. How should you classify that suggestion?\" explanation=\"CORS governs cross-origin requests between different servers. A same-folder local image is not a cross-origin request, so a CORS server config cannot apply. Fluency is not accuracy: reject the suggestion on the match check and record the reason, which is exactly the judgment the section is testing.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Reject: CORS governs cross-origin requests and cannot apply to a same-folder local file\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Accept: it sounds technical and the AI is usually right about configs\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Modify: keep the CORS idea but point it at the local folder instead\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nMaria's nav looks fine, but everything below it renders blue and underlined. She debugs with AI as a partner, not an oracle, and logs each suggestion.\n\n**Step 1. Read the symptom in the code.**\n\n```html\n<nav>\n  <a href=\"projects.html\">Projects</a>\n  <a href=\"about.html\">About\n</nav>\n<p>Welcome to my portfolio.</p>\n```\n\nThe About `<a>` is never closed. The open link swallows the content that follows, so the welcome paragraph below the nav renders as part of the link.\n\n**Step 2. Write a verifiable prompt.** Not \"fix my site.\" Instead: \"Every paragraph below my nav renders blue and underlined as if it were a link. Here is my nav block and the first paragraph. What in my markup causes this?\"\n\n**Step 3. Log the AI critique with a decision.**\n\n| AI suggestion | Decision | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Close the open anchor: add `</a>` after About | Accept | Matches the missing-close-tag symptom exactly |\n| Also add a CORS server config for the headshot | Reject | The image is a same-folder local file; CORS cannot apply here |\n| Rewrite the whole nav in a framework | Reject | Out of scope; the bug is one tag |\n\n**Step 4. Verify, do not assume.** Add `</a>`, reload, and check the browser console: the runaway styling is gone and no errors remain.\n\nThe fix was a single closing tag. The accepted-modified-rejected log shows judgment: Maria kept the suggestion that fit the evidence and rejected the off-target CORS advice because it could not apply to a local file.\n\nOrder the supervised-debugging steps so you read the evidence before you trust any suggestion. Identifying the symptom yourself comes first; verifying in the browser comes last.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Read the symptom in the code: runaway blue means an unclosed anchor tag\",\"Write a verifiable prompt that includes the symptom and the actual nav block\",\"Classify each AI suggestion as accept, modify, or reject with a reason\",\"Apply only the suggestion that passes the match and scope checks\",\"Reload and check the browser console to verify, never assume\",\"Record every decision and reason in the AI critique log\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor every AI suggestion, run it through three questions before touching your code:\n\n1. **Match check:** Does this fix address the symptom I actually observed? An unclosed-tag fix matches runaway styling; a server config does not match a local-file problem.\n2. **Scope check:** Is this proportionate to the bug? A one-tag bug does not justify a framework rewrite. Reject fixes that solve more than you asked.\n3. **Verify check:** After applying, did I confirm the result in the browser (and console), or did I just assume it worked?\n\nA suggestion that passes match and scope gets applied; then the verify check decides whether it stays. Anything failing match or scope is rejected with a reason, and the reason goes in your log.\n\nRun the AI suggestion to rewrite the whole nav in a JavaScript framework through the three questions and predict each verdict before you reveal it. The actual bug is one unclosed anchor tag.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Judge the framework-rewrite suggestion with the match, scope, and verify checks\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Match check first: does rewriting the nav in a framework address the observed symptom (runaway blue from one unclosed tag)?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, a framework would re-render the nav and clear the styling\",\"No, the symptom is a single missing close tag, which the rewrite does not target\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The symptom is one unclosed anchor. A framework rewrite does not address that specific cause directly; it replaces working code to chase a one-tag bug.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Scope check next: is a full framework rewrite proportionate to a one-tag bug?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, modernizing is always worth it\",\"No, it solves far more than was asked and adds large overhead\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A framework is absurd overhead for a missing </a>. The fix should be as small as the bug, so this fails the scope check.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Verdict: reject the framework rewrite on the scope check (and weakly on match), and log the reason. Accept only the close-tag fix, then run the verify check by reloading and confirming the console is clean.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading is treating AI output as correct because it is fluent. Confidence is not accuracy. A suggestion can be grammatically perfect, technically worded, and completely irrelevant to your bug, like recommending CORS for an image that lives in the same folder.\n\nA second misreading is logging only what you accepted. The rejections carry the most evidence of judgment, because choosing not to follow a confident suggestion, and saying why, is exactly the skill the section is testing.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to the nav bug. The student first reads the symptom and finds the `<a href=\"about.html\">About` link is never closed; the open link swallows the content after the nav, which explains why every paragraph below it renders as part of a link. Now armed with the actual cause, he evaluates the three AI suggestions. \"Add `</a>` after About\" passes the match check (it fixes the exact symptom) and the scope check (it is one tag), so he accepts it. \"Add a CORS server config for the headshot\" fails the match check (the image is a same-folder local file; CORS governs cross-origin requests, which do not apply), so he rejects it. \"Rewrite the nav in a framework\" fails the scope check (a framework is absurd overhead for one missing tag), so he rejects it.\n\nHe applies the closing tag, reloads, and runs the verify check: the runaway styling is gone and the console shows no errors. His log records all three decisions with reasons, so a reviewer can see he understood why the wrong suggestions were wrong, not just that the page now works.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader evaluates suggestions against evidence rather than against how authoritative they sound. As you study the worked example, cover the Decision column in the log and predict, for each suggestion, whether you would accept or reject it and on which check it turns. If your reasoning matches the log, you can supervise an assistant rather than be steered by one.\n\nA strong reader of AI-assisted debugging can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the difference between a prompt the AI can verify against and one it must guess at?\n2. Why is a fluent, confident suggestion not necessarily a correct one?\n3. For a given bug, which suggestions fail the match check and which fail the scope check?\n4. Why does the log of rejected suggestions show more skill than the list of accepted ones?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour debugging log is strong when a reader can reconstruct your reasoning for each AI suggestion: what the symptom was, what the assistant proposed, what you decided, and the specific reason. The log should make clear that you, not the model, made the call, and that you verified the outcome rather than trusting it.\n\nA strong submission highlights the suggestion you were most tempted to follow and chose not to: \"The CORS fix sounded technical and convincing, but I rejected it because my image is a local same-folder file, so cross-origin rules cannot apply.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same supervision method to a different tool and a different bug, say an AI assistant suggesting an Excel formula that returns the wrong total. The medium changes from HTML to a spreadsheet, but the method is identical: read the symptom (the total is off by one row), write a prompt that includes the formula and the wrong result, then judge the suggestion against the evidence (does the proposed range actually cover all rows?) and verify by checking the recomputed total. The accept-modify-reject log works the same way. Carrying the method across tools is the proof that you learned to supervise AI, not to debug one specific page.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-05-debugging-with-ai-assistance-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 06.05. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- MDN Web Docs: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS). https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CORS\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"Your nav looks fine, but every paragraph below it renders blue and underlined. The last nav link reads <a href=\"about.html\">About with no closing tag before </nav>. What is the actual bug?","options":["The browser is broken","The <a> was never closed with </a>, so the browser keeps treating the content after the nav as part of the link","GitHub Pages does not allow links","You used the wrong color in CSS"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A missing </a> leaves the link open, so the following content is swallowed into the anchor. Closing the tag fixes the runaway styling.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_browser_blamed_for_markup_bug","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance"],"stem":"Which prompt to an AI debugging assistant is most likely to get a useful, verifiable answer?","options":["My website is broken, fix it","My nav links show as plain text. Here is my index.html nav block and my style.css 'nav a' rule. What in this code prevents the styles from applying?","Make my site better","Why does HTML hate me"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A good debugging prompt states the symptom, pastes the relevant code, and asks a specific question. That lets you check the answer against your own files.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_vague_prompt_expectation","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance"],"stem":"An AI says your image will not load because of a CORS policy and gives you a long server config. Your image is a local file headshot.jpg in the same folder. What is the most likely real cause, and the right response?","options":["Apply the server config; the AI must be right","Distrust the off-target answer: a same-folder local image has no CORS issue, so check the filename, case, and src path first","Remove all images from the site","Move the whole project to a different country"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI sometimes diagnoses a problem you do not have. A local same-folder image cannot trigger CORS, so the realistic cause is a path or filename typo. Judge the suggestion against your context.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_ai_diagnosis_unverified","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_debugging_with_ai_assistance_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_debugging_with_ai_assistance","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"After AI suggests a fix, what single tool best confirms whether the fix actually resolved the error before you commit it?","options":["Reading the AI response again more carefully","Opening the browser developer console to see whether the error message is gone after reloading","Asking the AI if it is sure","Counting the lines of code"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The browser console shows real errors. Reloading and confirming the message disappeared is direct evidence the fix worked, independent of the AI's confidence.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_fix_assumed_without_check","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_browser_blamed_for_markup_bug","pattern":"blaming_tool_for_own_markup_error","description":"Attributing a rendering bug to the browser or platform when the real cause is an unclosed or malformed tag in the learner's own HTML","trigger":"learner sees runaway styling and concludes the browser is broken rather than inspecting their tags","feedback":"Most layout breaks are unbalanced tags. Read the markup around the symptom and check that each element that opens also closes.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_vague_prompt_expectation","pattern":"vague_debug_prompt","description":"Expecting a useful debugging answer from a vague prompt like fix my site instead of stating the symptom and pasting the relevant code","trigger":"learner asks the AI to fix it without describing the error or sharing code","feedback":"Give the AI the symptom, the exact code block, and a specific question. A targeted prompt produces an answer you can verify against your files.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_ai_diagnosis_unverified","pattern":"ai_diagnosis_accepted_unverified","description":"Acting on an AI diagnosis that does not fit the situation, such as a CORS fix for a same-folder local image","trigger":"learner applies an off-target AI fix without checking whether the diagnosed cause is even possible in their context","feedback":"Test the diagnosis against your context. If the named cause cannot apply, ignore the fix and look at the simpler explanation, usually a path or filename typo.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_5_fix_assumed_without_check","pattern":"fix_assumed_to_work","description":"Committing an AI-suggested fix without reloading and confirming in the browser console that the error is actually gone","trigger":"learner pastes a fix and commits it based on the AI's confidence alone","feedback":"Reload and check the developer console. A cleared error message is the evidence the fix worked; the AI saying so is not.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"0815d0e6dede7b2feba6d89b3c1448d04d0b942f724ebbc595d1c57a9abd0524","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}